{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rabbitmq-server","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:11.241Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/rabbitmq-server/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/rabbitmq-server/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Rabbitmq Server image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Rabbitmq Server?\nRabbitMQ is a message broker: it accepts and forwards messages. You can think about it as a post office: when you put the mail that you want posting in a post box, you can be sure that the letter carrier will eventually deliver the mail to your recipient. In this analogy, RabbitMQ is a post box, a post office, and a letter carrier.\n\nSee: [Rabbitmq Server](https://www.rabbitmq.com/)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `3`\n- `3-cm2.0`\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running the Daemon\n\nOne of the important things to note about RabbitMQ is that it stores data based on what it calls the \"Node Name\", which defaults to the hostname. What this means for usage in Docker is that we should specify -h/--hostname explicitly for each daemon so that we don't get a random hostname and can keep track of our data:\n\n\tdocker run -d --hostname my-rabbit --name some-rabbit mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rabbitmq-server:3\n\nThis will start a RabbitMQ container listening on the default port of 5672. If you give that a minute, then do `docker logs some-rabbit`, then you'll get an info output block on the running node.\n\n#### Environment Variables\n\nFor a list of environment variables supported by RabbitMQ itself, see the Environment Variables section of [rabbitmq.com/configure](https://www.rabbitmq.com/configure.html#supported-environment-variables)\n\n#### Erlang Cookie\n\nSee the [RabbitMQ \"Clustering Guide\"](https://www.rabbitmq.com/clustering.html#erlang-cookie) for more information about cookies and why they're necessary. For setting a consistent cookie (especially useful for clustering but also for remote/cross-container administration via `rabbitmqctl`), provide a cookie file (default location of `/var/lib/rabbitmq/.erlang.cookie`).\n\nFor example, you can provide the cookie via a file (such as with [Docker Secrets](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/secrets/)):\n\n\tdocker service create ... --secret source=my-erlang-cookie,target=/var/lib/rabbitmq/.erlang.cookie ... rabbitmq\n\n(Note that it will likely also be necessary to specify `uid=XXX,gid=XXX,mode=0600` in order for Erlang in the container to be able to read the cookie file properly. See [Docker's --secret documentation for more details](https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/service_create/#create-a-service-with-secrets).)\n\n### Launch Mariner Shell\n\nThe rabbitmq-server image ships with a bash shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n\tdocker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rabbitmq-server:3 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T16:54:01.5077821+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"206K+","stars":null},"mediaTypes":[],"contentTypes":[],"storage":null,"tags":[{"name":"3.11-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:a42fdf5cc3ba96dede3b261f30482f138cd4c1071c0a5fb91ccdf36fe183830f","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:14:36.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3.11","digest":"sha256:a42fdf5cc3ba96dede3b261f30482f138cd4c1071c0a5fb91ccdf36fe183830f","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:14:29.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:a42fdf5cc3ba96dede3b261f30482f138cd4c1071c0a5fb91ccdf36fe183830f","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:14:18.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3","digest":"sha256:a42fdf5cc3ba96dede3b261f30482f138cd4c1071c0a5fb91ccdf36fe183830f","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:14:08.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3.11.24-3-cm2.0.20260311","digest":"sha256:a42fdf5cc3ba96dede3b261f30482f138cd4c1071c0a5fb91ccdf36fe183830f","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:14: